# Line endings — normalise everything to LF in the repository.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# Without it, an editor saving a file with Windows line endings makes git see
# EVERY LINE as changed even when not a single character differs. That is exactly
# what happened here: `git status` showed 37 files modified and `git diff` counted
# 4503 insertions / 4503 deletions, while `git diff --ignore-cr-at-eol` came back
# completely empty. One careless `git add .` would have produced a commit burying
# the real change under thousands of phantom lines, and permanently broken
# `git blame` for those files.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` means: git decides what is text, stores it with LF, and
# checks it out with LF. Working copies stay consistent across machines.
* text=auto eol=lf

